7592186045436] [17592186050955 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428293 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050955 #edb/ref 1031 "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050955 #edb/ref 1033 1682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050955 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050955 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "754db256-ffbf-5395-b091-88cb18d8b674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093854 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093854 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093854 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093854 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8bd32982-99ac-5951-be9c-a7d6abcdb3a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186095826 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "93fc0622-6b89-5e60-84fd-c344c741bb85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096562 #edb/ref 1069 "e-745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096562 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)=e^{x}$ and let $P_2(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when $P_2(x)$ is used to approximate $e^{1.1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c946c1c9-f3d2-5d9d-9390-8ee542ca15a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096562 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_2$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial $P_2$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}|x-1|^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|f^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=1.1.$ Plugging $x=1.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|1.1-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|f^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,1.1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|f^{(3)}(x)|=e^x$ is an increasing function on the interval $[1,1.1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.1.$ So\n\n$$\nM = |f^{(3)}(1.1)| =e^{1.1},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{e^{1.1}}{6}|0.1|^{3}   \\approx 0.000\\,501,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096563 #edb/ref 1069 "e-746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096563 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)=\\ln(1-x)$ and let $P_3(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when $P_3(x)$ is used to approximate $\\ln(0.9)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f5e5945-cdaa-55b9-b360-9e68249480b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096563 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_3$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial $P_3$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|R_3| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{4!}|x-0|^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|f^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.1.$ Plugging $x=0.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|0.1-0{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|0.1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|f^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\,\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\,\\frac{6}{(x-1)^{4}} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|-\\,\\frac{6}{(x-1)^{4}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(x-1)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|f^{(4)}(x)|=\\dfrac{6}{(x-1)^4}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[0,0.1],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.1.$ So\n\n$$\nM = |f^{(4)}(0.1)| =\\dfrac{6}{(0.1-1)^4} = \\dfrac{6}{(0.9)^4},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|R_3| \\leq \\dfrac{ \\left( \\dfrac{6}{(0.9)^4} \\right) }{24}|0.1|^{4}  \\approx 0.000\\,038,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096820 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096821 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1045 "The Lagrange Error Bound" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096869 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f055ed71-ae5b-569a-8cbe-acd4850a5cce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-11585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate ${e}^{0.65}.$ Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f0c0766-f92b-5029-93d1-2aeb7633b5b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096980 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-1{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $x=0.65$ and $a=1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.65$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|0.65-1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|-0.35{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0.65,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}|={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{x}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[0.65,1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(1)|={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{e}{24}|-0.35{|}^{4}≈0.001\\,7,\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=2.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7bfbcc6e-0a0e-51f6-8574-b8c524edf820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097317 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-2{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=2$ and $x=2.3.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2.3$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}\\,{|2.3-2|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}\\,{|0.3|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[2,2.3].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}|=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[2,2.3],$ its value is greatest at $x=2.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(2)|=\\frac{2}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{6}\\,{|0.3|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{0.3}^{3}}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8\\,000}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1061 4844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c93a74b3-e513-5fb9-9595-6bf177ec940a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1061 4845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82542d7a-3ec1-5979-8dd2-13a732ad5eb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1061 4849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13415159-b315-5389-bc81-61ef044820e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097754 #edb/ref 1061 4850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097754 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b70fcb39-7efc-50ad-a28c-73509579c61d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097754 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to *approximate* $f(x) = e^x$ for some value of $x.$ We can do this using the second-degree Taylor polynomial about a point $x=a.$ For example, the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $e^x$ about the point $x=1$ is shown below:\n\n$$\ne^x \\approx P_2(x) = e + e(x-1) + \\dfrac 1 2 e(x-1)^2\n$$\n\nIn general, the polynomial $P_2(x)$ is only an approximation to the function $f(x),$ which means that there will usually be some error whenever we use $P_2$ to approximate $f.$ We want to know how bad this error can get. Can we find an upper bound for the error? \n\nLet $R_2(x)$ be the **error** (or **remainder**) that we get when we use $P_2$ to approximate $f(x)=e^x,$ so \n\n$$\nR_2(x) = e^x  - P_2(x).\n$$\n\nThe **Lagrange error bound** states that an upper bound for $R_2$ is given by,\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}|x-a|^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ the maximum value of $|f^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a$ and $x.$ \n\nIn our case, since our Taylor polynomial is taken about the point $a=1,$ the Lagrange error bound becomes\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}|x-1|^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the Lagrange error bound looks a lot like the last term of the *next* Taylor polynomial, $P_3(x).$\n\nThe tricky part is finding the value of $M.$ We'll learn how to do that in the following example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1065 1292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fcb4053a-c61a-538b-8b44-5a92f73f053f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1064 "The Lagrange error bound that we've discussed so far is valid only for the error associated with a second-degree Taylor polynomial. But we can generalize the result to higher-order Taylor polynomials.\n\nLet $R_n(x)$ be the error involved in approximating a function $f(x)$ by its $n$th-degree Taylor polynomial $P_n(x)$ about $x=a.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nR_n(x) = f(x) - P_n(x).\n$$\n\nThen the Lagrange error bound for $R_n$ is given by\n\n$$\n|R_n(x)| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{(n+1)!}|x-a|^{n+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|f^{(n+1)}|$ on the interval between $a$ and $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1065 1294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "07b10e6a-1673-5d6e-8572-8b4505579f44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1067 "The General Result of the Lagrange Error Bound" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529582 #edb/ref 1080 "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{2}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "028fcae0-cbd2-5656-994f-f49bd8d68fde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529612 #edb/ref 1080 "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1