86045417] [17592186530394 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530394 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530394 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530394 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530394 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eedf09a2-2732-57b4-bde6-4d08d850c1fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530415 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530415 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\sqrt{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "009bfd07-1f0d-5688-8e73-36cc8b112d6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530415 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-1{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=3.$ \n\nPlugging $x=3$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|3-1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|2{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,3].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2}|=\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2}=\\frac{15}{16{x}^{7/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|=\\frac{15}{16{x}^{7/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,3],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(1)|=\\frac{15}{16(1)^{7/2}}=\\frac{15}{16},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{15}{16})}{24}|2{|}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot {2}^{4}}{16\\cdot 24} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530446 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530446 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530446 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530446 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d9e0c2d-cfb3-5637-bf5c-58e8b1c970c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530446 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-1{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|2-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,2].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-\\frac{15}{8}{x}^{-7/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}|=\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,2],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(1)|=\\frac{15}{8(1)^{7/2}}=\\frac{15}{8},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{15}{8})}{6}|1{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16} \\\\  & ≈0.313\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nup to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530492 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530492 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103856" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530492 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530492 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate ${e}^{-3.3}={e}^{-3(1.1)}.$ Round your answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530492 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "948d9306-7c7c-59c6-bfff-2f8bea699654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530492 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-1{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=1.1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=1.1$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|1.1-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-3{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-27{e}^{-3x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-27{e}^{-3x}|=27{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=27{e}^{-3x}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,1.1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(1)|=27{e}^{-3(1)}=\\frac{27}{{e}^{3}},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{27}{{e}^{3}})}{6}|0.1{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2{e}^{3}}\\cdot 0.001 \\\\  & ≈0.0002.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530503 #edb/ref 1069 "q-13972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530503 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-x}$ about $x=0$ to approximate the value of the number $\\frac{1}{e}.$ Find the Lagrange error bound for this approximation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6eeece9-3543-5575-94c0-d71f28347d32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530503 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-0{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=1$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|1-0{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{-x}|={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{-x}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,1],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(0)|={e}^{-0}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}|1{|}^{4}=\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530512 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)=(1+x)^{3/2}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $(1.9)^{3/2}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5e88433-85a5-5481-9536-7dfae4f91322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530512 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-0{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.9.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.9$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|0.9-0{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.9{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.9].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1+x)^{3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}(1+x)^{1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}(1+x)^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{8}(1+x)^{-3/2} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{3}{8}(1+x)^{-3/2}|=\\frac{3}{8(1+x)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{3}{8(1+x)^{3/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.9],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(0)|=\\frac{3}{8(1+0)^{3/2}}=\\frac{3}{8},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{6}|0.9{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.9)^{3}}{16} \\\\  & ≈0.046.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530524 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530524 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530524 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530524 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)={e}^{-x}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}={e}^{-0.5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530524 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c81fea43-7a0e-5b24-b7d9-362bddbf7de1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530524 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-0{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.5.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.5$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|0.5-0{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.5{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.5].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-{e}^{-x}|={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|={e}^{-x}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.5],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(0)|=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{1}{6}|0.5{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot {2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530534 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{e}}={e}^{-1/3}.$ Round your answer to $5$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e46215f5-878f-5a8e-bbfe-fcdc781327b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530534 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-0{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $a=0.$ \n\nPlugging $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}{|-\\frac{1}{3}-0|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{3},0].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}|={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{x}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{3},0],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(0)|={e}^{0}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{1}{24}{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24\\cdot {3}^{4}} \\\\  & ≈0.000\\,51,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530544 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530544 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530544 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc2772cd-2bc4-5ed7-b988-82cf9e52af83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530544 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-0{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=1$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|1-0{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,1],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(0)|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}|1{|}^{4}=\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$" #edb/ref 1759218